# Test files that may add sleeps, for tools/check/test-sleeps.py: <path> | <reason>. A file named here gets its count
# recorded by --update (and raised by it later); every other file's count only goes down. The reason says why the test
# needs a real wait (for example a timer or an idle timeout against the real clock) instead of waiting for an event.
kuicksilver-http3/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/http3/runtime/HeaderLimitsTest.kt | aHeadInTimeWithASlowBodyIsServed, aLateButWholeResponseIsNoSlowHead, negative_aStalledTypeIsNeverStoppedWhileItMayBeACriticalStream: the field-section deadline is a timer on the real clock, and these show that what comes later than it (a body, a response, a critical stream's type) is not cut (no event marks "the deadline would have passed")
kuicksilver-ktor-client/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/client/ClientH2WebSocketTest.kt | negative_theTransportWaitCountsFromBothCloses: the server's late Close (1.6 s against the real 5 s close wait) is the behaviour under test
kuicksilver-ktor-client/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/client/ClientWebSocketCloseOrderTest.kt | negative_anUnwrittenCloseOverTls…: calls the shared `probing` helper, whose periodic probe byte makes macOS accept a reset sent while the server's receive window is 0
kuicksilver-ktor-client/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/client/ClientWebSocketCloseTest.kt | `probing`: a probe byte every 100 ms (macOS ignores a reset to a socket with a zero receive window until it sends); the close wait against the real clock is the behaviour under test
kuicksilver-ktor-network/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/network/IdleSelectorCloseTest.kt | the selector must go idle for real (2 s pause, writer stuck for 300 ms) before the abort; the peer's periodic probe byte as in `probing`
kuicksilver-ktor-network/src/jvmTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/network/ReleaseCoalescingTest.kt | polls an internal per-selector counter that has no event, bounded at 10 s
kuicksilver-ktor-client/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/client/RequestDnsTest.kt | negative_anAnswerAtTheEndOfTheResolutionDelayIsUsed: a delay in virtual time (runTest), not a real wait: the lookup must end at the same virtual instant as the Resolution Delay
kuicksilver-native/src/c/test/test_udp.c | queue_burst: Darwin counts the datagrams queued on a socket (SO_NUMRCVPKT) but signals none of the counts (POLLIN fires at the first), so the wait for a whole burst polls the count every 5 ms, bounded at 5 s; loopback delivery is asynchronous there and drops datagrams under a flood; fd_probe_child: a forged ICMP message is handled in a softirq with nothing to wait on, so the positive control polls a DO socket for EMSGSIZE every 10 ms (2 s bound), and the AF_PACKET capture and the non-blocking receive poll every 10 ms (100 ms and 1 s bounds)
kuicksilver-ktor-server/src/jvmTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/server/StopFirstRequestJvmTest.kt | negative_aFirstRequestThatComesJustAfterTheStopBeganIsAnswered: an end-to-end smoke test over a real socket (the request comes 300 ms after the stop began, inside the 2 s window on the real clock), not the proof: H1PipelineSelectRaceTest covers the window in virtual time (answered inside it, closed after it, keep-alive closes at once)
kuicksilver-quic/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/quic/runtime/LoopGroupTest.kt | burst: polls the loops' hand-off and refusal counters, which only grow and have no event, until every handshake was decided, bounded at 20 s (an instant count read after a fixed 3 s wait failed under emulation); the other waits poll counters the same way, and one gives a loop that starts too early its chance; SlowIo delays every receive of a late-reading client on purpose (the refusal reaches it after the server decided)
kuicksilver-quic/src/nativeTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/quic/KtorDatagramIoTest.kt | the refused-binds test reads the descriptor count until it has stopped moving, bounded at 10 s: the selector loops of binds that already returned close their pipes when a thread takes them, which nothing signals, and a baseline taken before they did would hide a later round's leak; after a failed count it waits 5 s more and reports what is left, so a handle that ends late is told apart from a leak
kuicksilver-ktor-client/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/client/ClientH3StallTest.kt | slowServerIsNoStall, idleLongLivedResponseIsNoStall, pingsComeWithinHalfThePeersIdleTimeout, nonRepeatableRequestSurvivesAShortOutage, aWebSocketOutlivesAStallThatRecovered, aResponseThatArrivedWholeSurvivesAStall, noWatcherMeansNoPollsAndNoPings, aStallHoldsTheSocketTimeoutUntilThePathComesBack: the server's think time, the gap between two events, an outage's length and a span without a watcher are the behaviour under test (seconds of real time against the stall thresholds and idle timeouts, which no event can stand for: the absence of wake-ups and PINGs has no event)
kuicksilver-ktor-client/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/client/FaultyIo.kt | FaultyIo.receive: receiveDelay holds received datagrams that long to make a path's RTT longer (the RTT is the behaviour under test); off unless a test sets it
kuicksilver-ktor-network/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/network/TlsSocketCloseTest.kt | aServerCloseStillDeliversTheLastResponseToASlowUploadingClient: a slow reader (2 ms per 64 KiB read) is the behaviour under test: the server's close must begin while most of its last response is still in flight behind the client, which no event marks, and a reader paced by an event would either read it all first or hold the server's writes
kuicksilver-ktor-server/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/server/ServerEchTest.kt | handshake: polls SessionCache.size() every 5 ms until the server's six tickets are stored (no event marks a stored ticket), bounded by the handshake's 10 s withTimeout; it makes the ordering the Windows leak needed (tickets read before the socket closes) happen on every run
kuicksilver-ktor-client/src/jvmFirstScreenTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/guide/quickstart/QuickstartClient.kt | the quickstart's client as the guide shows it (compiled, never run): its pause between requests lets HTTP/3 take over, which is what the reader is meant to see
kuicksilver-ktor-client/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/client/ClientSocketTimeoutTest.kt | aSlowButProgressingDownloadNeverTimesOut, aSlowButProgressingUploadNeverTimesOut, eventStreamsAreLeftAlone, webSocketSessionsAreLeftAlone, httpTimeoutTakesPrecedence, nullTurnsItOff, negative_anExplicitHttpTimeoutStillAppliesToEventStreams, negative_anEventStreamNobodyAskedForKeepsTheTimeout: the server routes these share pause twice the 2 s socket timeout or move every 100 ms for 3 s, end to end against the engine's real clock over HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3; the timer logic itself runs under virtual time in InactivityTest
kuicksilver-ktor-client/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/client/InactivityTest.kt | aHoldStopsTheTimerAndItStartsOverAfter, aBlockThatEndsDuringAHoldIsNoTimeout, aBodyThatKeepsMovingNeverTimesOut, negative_aStalledBodyTimesOutAndAHoldDefersIt: delays under runTest's virtual time (no real wait): they place a stall, a hold and steady progress at exact instants of the socket timeout's clock
kuicksilver-ktor-client/src/commonTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/client/ClientSseTest.kt | the servers pace their event streams (an event every 100 ms, a 1 s pause) and the slow consumer reads slower than its server writes: an event stream that stays open across real pauses is the behaviour under test; sessionCollectedLate and h2SessionCancelledUncollectedResetsTheStream wait before they read so that Ktor has completed the call meanwhile (HttpStatement.cleanup runs in a coroutine of its own, and nothing signals its end)
kuicksilver-ktor-server/src/jvmTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/server/browser/WtCodesProbe.kt | writeUntilStopped: the browser page's probe writes 1 KiB every 20 ms on a stream the browser cancelled until its STOP_SENDING, which the session API reports only to a write; a real browser on the real clock; unpaced, it sent Firefox (which sends no STOP_SENDING) 12.8 MB of stream credit per cancelled stream, and before the transport fix its writer then filled the server's shared send buffer and stalled the rest of the session (docs/spec/interop.md)
kuicksilver-ktor-server/src/jvmTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/server/browser/ChromeWebTransportTest.kt | serves the browser page through WtCodesProbe.routes, whose writeUntilStopped paces its writes for a real browser (see WtCodesProbe.kt)
kuicksilver-ktor-server/src/jvmTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/ktor/server/browser/ManualWebTransportServer.kt | serves the browser page through WtCodesProbe.routes, whose writeUntilStopped paces its writes for a real browser (see WtCodesProbe.kt)
kuicksilver-quic/src/jvmTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/quic/api/DatagramFairnessJvmTest.kt | a paced sender (20 ms apart) against another thread's datagram flood through the real endpoint loop: the pacing against a CPU-speed flood is the behaviour under test, which no event can stand for
kuicksilver-quic/src/jvmTest/kotlin/io/kuicksilver/quic/api/DatagramDrainerLatencyJvmTest.kt | probes the endpoint loop every 50 ms on the real clock while other threads flood datagrams: the loop's latency under a CPU-speed flood is the behaviour under test, which no event can stand for
